  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThere have been some minor shifts in the global model guidance \nfor late this week that result in increased rain chances for\nsoutheast portions of the area. The El Nino-enhanced subtropical \njet stream is becoming more active, and models have trended more \npronounced with an upper level perturbation crossing the Gulf \nCoast region. The model consensus suggests a brief period of \ncyclonic flow and moisture transport will increase rain chances in\nroughly the Thursday to Saturday time frame, mainly along the \nI-59 corridor. With that said, we will stay conservative with\nrainfall forecast amounts given the long term drought. \n \nOtherwise, anticyclonic flow will primarily drive our weather \nthrough this weekend and into early next week. Models remain weak \nand and less amplified with a shortwave trough crossing the Plains\nto MS Valley region this weekend in the mid latitude Pacific \nstream, and the prospects for any significant rainfall continue to\nbe very low. Given the extremely dry conditions so far this fall \nseason, we will be monitoring wind/RH forecasts closely for \nincreased wildfire danger. /EC/",
